{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GFkwEUH2Jk7Aztzv1oQuuH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd055-tail\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/tail: Print the Last Lines or Bytes of Files\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  tail is the complement to head: it extracts the end of files or follows growing files\n6\t  in real time. The macOS BSD tail differs from GNU tail in byte-mode support, plus-prefix\n7\t  semantics, and multi-file header formatting. The Go implementation must match the\n8\t  Homebrew GNU reference binary (gtail, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all\n9\t  static flag combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils. Follow\n10\t  mode (-f) is excluded from differential testing because its output is non-deterministic.\n11\t\n12\tgoals:\n13\t  - G1: Print the last N lines of each input file, defaulting to 10 lines.\n14\t  - G2: Support byte-count mode (-c) as an alternative to line-count mode (-n).\n15\t  - G3: Support starting from a given line or byte offset with the + prefix.\n16\t  - G4: Verify functional parity against gtail via differential testing on static modes.\n17\t\n18\trequirements:\n19\t  R1:\n20\t    title: Line-count mode (default and -n)\n21\t    items:\n22\t      - R1.1:\n23\t          text: When no flags are given, must print the last 10 lines of each input file.\n24\t      - R1.2:\n25\t          text: \"-n NUM or --lines=NUM must print the last NUM lines. NUM must be a positive integer or prefixed with '+' to start from line NUM.\"\n26\t      - R1.3:\n27\t          text: \"When NUM is prefixed with '+' (e.g., -n +5), must print starting from line NUM to the end of the file. Line numbering starts at 1.\"\n28\t      - R1.4:\n29\t          text: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given or when a file argument is \"-\".\n30\t\n31\t  R2:\n32\t    title: Byte-count mode (-c)\n33\t    items:\n34\t      - R2.1:\n35\t          text: \"-c NUM or --bytes=NUM must print the last NUM bytes of each input. -c and -n are mutually exclusive; the last one given takes precedence.\"\n36\t      - R2.2:\n37\t          text: \"When NUM is prefixed with '+' (e.g., -c +100), must print starting from byte NUM to the end. Byte numbering starts at 1.\"\n38\t      - R2.3:\n39\t          text: NUM may use multiplier suffixes as defined by GNU coreutils (b, K/KiB, M/MiB, G/GiB).\n40\t\n41\t  R3:\n42\t    title: Multi-file output and headers\n43\t    items:\n44\t      - R3.1:\n45\t          text: \"When multiple files are given, must precede each file's output with a header in the format '==> FILENAME <==' followed by a newline. A blank line separates each file's header from the previous file's output.\"\n46\t      - R3.2:\n47\t          text: When a single file is given, must not print a header by default.\n48\t      - R3.3:\n49\t          text: -q or --quiet or --silent must suppress all headers, even for multiple files.\n50\t      - R3.4:\n51\t          text: -v or --verbose must print headers even for a single file.\n52\t\n53\t  R4:\n54\t    title: Exit codes and differential testing\n55\t    items:\n56\t      - R4.1:\n57\t          text: Must exit 0 when all input files are processed successfully.\n58\t      - R4.2:\n59\t          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened or read. Must still process and output content for the files that were successfully read.\n60\t      - R4.3:\n61\t          text: \"Differential tests must compare stdout output and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils. Tests must cover: default 10 lines, explicit -n count, -c byte count, +N offset (lines and bytes), multi-file headers, -q, -v, stdin input, and error on non-existent file.\"\n62\t      - R4.4:\n63\t          text: \"When a named file cannot be opened, must print an error message to stderr and continue processing remaining files.\"\n64\t\n65\tnon_goals:\n66\t  - \"cmd/tail does not implement -f or --follow (follow mode). Follow mode output is non-deterministic and cannot be verified by differential testing.\"\n67\t  - \"cmd/tail does not implement --pid (terminate after process dies). This flag is only meaningful with -f.\"\n68\t  - \"cmd/tail does not implement --retry (keep trying to open a file). This flag is only meaningful with -f.\"\n69\t  - cmd/tail does not implement -F (equivalent to --follow=name --retry).\n70\t\n71\tpackage_contract:\n72\t  imports:\n73\t    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sizeparse\n74\t    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n75\t\n76\tacceptance_criteria:\n77\t  - id: AC1\n78\t    criterion: \"seq 1 20 | go run ./cmd/tail produces lines 11 through 20, matching gtail.\"\n79\t    traces:\n80\t      - R1.1\n81\t      - R1.2\n82\t      - R1.3\n83\t      - R1.4\n84\t  - id: AC2\n85\t    criterion: \"seq 1 20 | go run ./cmd/tail -n 5 produces lines 16 through 20, matching gtail.\"\n86\t    traces:\n87\t      - R1.2\n88\t      - R1.3\n89\t      - R2.1\n90\t      - R4.3\n91\t  - id: AC3\n92\t    criterion: \"seq 1 20 | go run ./cmd/tail -n +5 produces lines 5 through 20, matching gtail.\"\n93\t    traces:\n94\t      - R1.2\n95\t      - R1.3\n96\t      - R2.1\n97\t      - R4.3\n98\t  - id: AC4\n99\t    criterion: \"printf 'abcdefghij' | go run ./cmd/tail -c 5 produces 'fghij', matching gtail.\"\n100\t    traces:\n101\t      - R2.1\n102\t      - R2.2\n103\t      - R4.3\n104\t  - id: AC5\n105\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/tail file1.txt file2.txt prints headers and correct tail content, matching gtail.\"\n106\t    traces:\n107\t      - R1.1\n108\t      - R1.2\n109\t      - R1.3\n110\t      - R1.4\n111\t  - id: AC6\n112\t    criterion: \"Differential test against gtail passes for all static flag combinations listed in R4.3.\"\n113\t    traces:\n114\t      - R4.3\n115\t  - id: AC7\n116\t    criterion: \"cmd/tail compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n117\t    traces:\n118\t      - R1.1\n119\t      - R1.2\n120\t      - R1.3\n121\t      - R1.4\n122\t      - R2.1\n123\t      - R2.2\n124\t      - R2.3\n125\t      - R3.1\n126\t      - R3.2\n127\t      - R3.3\n128\t      - R3.4\n129\t      - R4.1\n130\t      - R4.2\n131\t      - R4.3\n132\t      - R4.4\n133\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7572528e-a6da-4fdd-8b4b-68f7c8392313","uuid":"e6e540d5-c9bf-4821-b72c-334fc4cfda46","timestamp":"2026-05-20T22:51:31.418Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5380/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd055-tail.yaml","content":"id: srd055-tail\ntitle: \"cmd/tail: Print the Last Lines or Bytes of Files\"\n\nproblem: |\n  tail is the complement to head: it extracts the end of files or follows growing files\n  in real time. The macOS BSD tail differs from GNU tail in byte-mode support, plus-prefix\n  semantics, and multi-file header formatting. The Go implementation must match the\n  Homebrew GNU reference binary (gtail, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all\n  static flag combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils. Follow\n  mode (-f) is excluded from differential testing because its output is non-deterministic.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Print the last N lines of each input file, defaulting to 10 lines.\n  - G2: Support byte-count mode (-c) as an alternative to line-count mode (-n).\n  - G3: Support starting from a given line or byte offset with the + prefix.\n  - G4: Verify functional parity against gtail via differential testing on static modes.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Line-count mode (default and -n)\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: When no flags are given, must print the last 10 lines of each input file.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: \"-n NUM or --lines=NUM must print the last NUM lines. NUM must be a positive integer or prefixed with '+' to start from line NUM.\"\n      - R1.3:\n          text: \"When NUM is prefixed with '+' (e.g., -n +5), must print starting from line NUM to the end of the file. Line numbering starts at 1.\"\n      - R1.4:\n          text: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given or when a file argument is \"-\".\n\n  R2:\n    title: Byte-count mode (-c)\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: \"-c NUM or --bytes=NUM must print the last NUM bytes of each input. -c and -n are mutually exclusive; the last one given takes precedence.\"\n      - R2.2:\n          text: \"When NUM is prefixed with '+' (e.g., -c +100), must print starting from byte NUM to the end. Byte numbering starts at 1.\"\n      - R2.3:\n          text: NUM may use multiplier suffixes as defined by GNU coreutils (b, K/KiB, M/MiB, G/GiB).\n\n  R3:\n    title: Multi-file output and headers\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: \"When multiple files are given, must precede each file's output with a header in the format '==> FILENAME <==' followed by a newline. A blank line separates each file's header from the previous file's output.\"\n      - R3.2:\n          text: When a single file is given, must not print a header by default.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: -q or --quiet or --silent must suppress all headers, even for multiple files.\n      - R3.4:\n          text: -v or --verbose must print headers even for a single file.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes and differential testing\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 when all input files are processed successfully.\n      - R4.2:\n          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened or read. Must still process and output content for the files that were successfully read.\n      - R4.3:\n          text: \"Differential tests must compare stdout output and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils. Tests must cover: default 10 lines, explicit -n count, -c byte count, +N offset (lines and bytes), multi-file headers, -q, -v, stdin input, and error on non-existent file.\"\n      - R4.4:\n          text: \"When a named file cannot be opened, must print an error message to stderr and continue processing remaining files.\"\n\nnon_goals:\n  - \"cmd/tail does not implement -f or --follow (follow mode). Follow mode output is non-deterministic and cannot be verified by differential testing.\"\n  - \"cmd/tail does not implement --pid (terminate after process dies). This flag is only meaningful with -f.\"\n  - \"cmd/tail does not implement --retry (keep trying to open a file). This flag is only meaningful with -f.\"\n  - cmd/tail does not implement -F (equivalent to --follow=name --retry).\n\npackage_contract:\n  imports:\n    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sizeparse\n    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"seq 1 20 | go run ./cmd/tail produces lines 11 through 20, matching gtail.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"seq 1 20 | go run ./cmd/tail -n 5 produces lines 16 through 20, matching gtail.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R2.1\n      - R4.3\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"seq 1 20 | go run ./cmd/tail -n +5 produces lines 5 through 20, matching gtail.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R2.1\n      - R4.3\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"printf 'abcdefghij' | go run ./cmd/tail -c 5 produces 'fghij', matching gtail.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R4.3\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/tail file1.txt file2.txt prints headers and correct tail content, matching gtail.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"Differential test against gtail passes for all static flag combinations listed in R4.3.\"\n    traces:\n      - R4.3\n  - id: AC7\n    criterion: \"cmd/tail compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.