ntax supports both `+5` (start from line 5) and `5` (last 5 lines), with a default of 10. I should also check whether the legacy `-NUM` shorthand is required by the specification.\n\nLooking at the requirements, it specifies `-n NUM` or `--lines=NUM` where NUM is either a positive integer or prefixed with `+`, so I'll skip the legacy form. For the `parseLineCount` function, I need to handle the `+` prefix differently than head does with negative numbers.\n\nWriting parseLineCount function...\n\nNow I'm thinking through edge cases like `tail -n 0` and `tail -n +0`, where the behavior depends on whether we're counting from the end or starting from a specific line number.\n\nThe SRD specifies that line numbering starts at 1 for the `+` prefix, so I'll stick to that specification rather than worrying about GNU tail quirks. Since we're doing differential testing against gtail anyway, any implementation differences will be caught by the tests.\n\nFor the core logic, I need to handle two cases: outputting the last N lines by buffering everything and then printing the tail, or skipping the first N-1 lines and outputting the rest when starting from a specific line number.\n\nWriting tail implementation... I'm reconsidering the approach—a simpler method would be to just load all the lines into memory and output the last N ones, which is more straightforward than managing a ring buffer.\n\nWriting tail functions...\n\nNow I'm thinking through the overall structure—keeping each function under 40 lines and the whole file under 500 lines. I should also verify edge cases like `-n 0` producing no output and `-n +1` outputting everything, which the differential tests will catch. For error handling, GNU tail formats messages like \"tail: cannot open 'nonexistent' for reading: No such file or directory\", but since we're discarding stderr in tests, matching the exit code is what matters. Go's `os.Open` error format should be close enough. the Go error directly to stderr, so I'll do the same. Now I'm ready to write the actual implementation, keeping in mind the constraints: functions under 40 lines, total file under 500 lines, plus I need to handle SIGPIPE and structure things for future extensions like byte mode. Writing main.go... Writing the file handling logic... Parsing command-line arguments by iterating through them, handling long flags with `--`, short flags with `-`, and collecting remaining arguments as files. Writing flag parsing logic... I'm verifying each function stays under the 40-line limit, and they all check out. Now I need to write differential tests for the test file that cover the core requirements around default behavior, explicit line counts with both flag formats, and the plus-prefix syntax.\n\nWriting test setup... Adding more test cases... Adding more test cases...\n\nNow I'm realizing I need to handle file-based tests for the R1.4 requirement about reading from stdin versus files. Single-file mode doesn't print headers like multi-file mode does, so I can test that behavior by passing a file argument instead of using stdin. Adding test cases for file arguments...\n\nNow I'm thinking about error handling — the requirements specify that the tool should exit with code 1 when files can't be opened or read, and I need to make sure I'm testing those failure scenarios too. I'm deciding whether to keep the same function signature as the existing test helpers or adapt it for the byte slices that my sequence function returns—I'll just convert at the call site to stay consistent with the established pattern. Let me finalize the test structure and make sure I have all the necessary imports for file operations.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how to handle negative numbers in the line count parsing, since GNU tail treats `-n -5` the same as `-n 5`. I need to check if the parsed value is negative and convert it to positive to match that behavior.\n\nParsing line count logic... Parsing number handling...\n\nI'm realizing that negative numbers like `-n -5` aren't covered in the test cases, so even though GNU tail accepts them and my implementation would output nothing, it won't cause a test failure. Since the spec says NUM must be positive or prefixed with '+', I'll leave the current parsing as-is and address GNU compatibility later if needed.\n\nNow I'm finalizing the code and checking my imports—I need bufio, fmt, io, os, strconv, and strings for main.go, plus the SIGPIPE handler from the sys package. For the test file, I'm verifying which standard library packages are actually being used.\n\nI'm reconsidering the processFile function structure. While head inlines its file handling logic, keeping it separate here makes the code cleaner and aligns with the requirement to structure for future extension—when headers get added later, the main loop will need to coordinate header printing based on file position and mode settings.\n\nLooking at how head delegates content processing with `processInput`, I should follow a similar pattern where main handles the file open/close lifecycle but delegates the actual line processing to a separate function like `tailLines`.\n\nWriting main function... Finishing up the main function and wrapping the program. The code looks good at 34 lines, well under the 40-line target. Now I need to consider whether to add `--help` and `--version` flags like the head implementation has, though I should check what's actually necessary for the requirements.\n\nLooking at the spec, it only covers R1.1 through R1.4, so I'll keep the implementation focused on those core features without the extra flags for now. They can be added later if needed. Since I'm not testing `--help` behavior, skipping it won't cause test failures.\n\nThe `--` separator is already handled in my argument parser, which is good since it's standard POSIX behavior. Now I need to write out the actual implementation files and verify the directory structure exists before creating them.\n\nI should only import `pkg/sys`